WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.450
&gt;&gt; So, hello everyone.

00:00:05.451 --> 00:00:06.745
My name is Dhrubojyoti.

00:00:06.746 --> 00:00:08.280
I'm a PhD student working with

00:00:08.281 --> 00:00:10.660
Professor Anish Arora at
Ohio State University.

00:00:10.661 --> 00:00:12.360
Before we tell you about

00:00:12.361 --> 00:00:13.920
what we explored as
part of this workshop,

00:00:13.921 --> 00:00:15.450
I would like to give
you an overview

00:00:15.451 --> 00:00:17.535
of the problem that we
were interested in.

00:00:17.536 --> 00:00:21.030
So, we have traditionally done

00:00:21.031 --> 00:00:22.380
shallow machine learning on

00:00:22.381 --> 00:00:24.350
mote scale and slightly larger,

00:00:24.351 --> 00:00:26.530
maybe ASOC scale devices.

00:00:26.531 --> 00:00:29.515
One of the devices that
we have developed is

00:00:29.516 --> 00:00:33.100
a 50 milliwatt Pulsed
Doppler Radar that

00:00:33.101 --> 00:00:36.335
is integrated with
a 64 megahertz Cortex M3 mote,

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:41.340
this is what we call the
bumblebee radar and a.NOW Board.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.740
When an object displaces
in front of the radar,

00:00:44.741 --> 00:00:46.960
even though it cannot
do absolute ranging,

00:00:46.961 --> 00:00:48.640
what it can do is
it can figure out

00:00:48.641 --> 00:00:51.070
the displacement of
the object and create what

00:00:51.071 --> 00:00:53.110
we call a cut for the duration of

00:00:53.111 --> 00:00:56.320
the object's displacement
that is visible by the radar.

00:00:56.321 --> 00:00:58.210
Through analysis of the cuts,

00:00:58.211 --> 00:00:59.545
we have traditionally designed

00:00:59.546 --> 00:01:02.070
some engineered features
in amplitude phase

00:01:02.071 --> 00:01:06.345
and joint time-frequency domain

00:01:06.346 --> 00:01:08.485
over the duration over
the displacement.

00:01:08.486 --> 00:01:11.685
For detection of
objects such as humans,

00:01:11.686 --> 00:01:14.775
cows, cars, et cetera,

00:01:14.776 --> 00:01:18.685
we have traditionally relied
on an RBF-kernel SVM using

00:01:18.686 --> 00:01:20.640
12 features down selected from

00:01:20.641 --> 00:01:23.790
a bigger list of
90 or 100 features.

00:01:23.791 --> 00:01:26.240
It currently has 94.6 percent

00:01:26.241 --> 00:01:28.860
accuracy for
object classification.

00:01:28.861 --> 00:01:32.150
However, it comes with
the huge overhead of A,

00:01:32.151 --> 00:01:33.410
storing the support vectors which

00:01:33.411 --> 00:01:35.585
alone consumes 30 kilobytes.

00:01:35.586 --> 00:01:38.255
B, feature computation in fact

00:01:38.256 --> 00:01:39.920
consumes more than 90 percent

00:01:39.921 --> 00:01:41.670
of the end-to-end
classification cycle.

00:01:41.671 --> 00:01:44.705
So if the classifier
is- we cannot really,

00:01:44.706 --> 00:01:46.310
our features are
such that we need

00:01:46.311 --> 00:01:47.840
a substantial length
of the cut to

00:01:47.841 --> 00:01:49.760
be processed in order to generate

00:01:49.761 --> 00:01:52.125
them because they are
statistics built on time.

00:01:52.126 --> 00:01:55.700
Therefore, 90 percent or more

00:01:55.701 --> 00:01:57.590
of the full classification cycle

00:01:57.591 --> 00:01:59.525
is consumed by
feature computation.

00:01:59.526 --> 00:02:01.070
So, these are essentially

00:02:01.071 --> 00:02:04.010
the two tall poles and we
were really motivated to try

00:02:04.011 --> 00:02:05.960
out feature learning
techniques instead of

00:02:05.961 --> 00:02:07.670
feature engineering techniques to

00:02:07.671 --> 00:02:09.315
see if we can mitigate them.

00:02:09.316 --> 00:02:11.955
Before coming here, we did
experiments with Bonsai,

00:02:11.956 --> 00:02:15.200
we found that it gave us
95.2 percent accuracy.

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.300
It only consume two kilobytes
of memory which was

00:02:17.301 --> 00:02:19.310
a big win in terms of
the classifier size,

00:02:19.311 --> 00:02:21.240
but the caveat was
still that we had

00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:23.550
to compute engineering features.

00:02:23.551 --> 00:02:25.810
The second application we are

00:02:25.811 --> 00:02:28.180
interested in along
with our partners at

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:30.130
New York University is deploying

00:02:30.131 --> 00:02:31.690
a sensor network comprised of

00:02:31.691 --> 00:02:33.375
the nodes that you see here.

00:02:33.376 --> 00:02:34.670
They are slightly bigger than

00:02:34.671 --> 00:02:36.370
the mote I showed you
in the previous slide.

00:02:36.371 --> 00:02:39.295
These are more like
variable scale but not quite.

00:02:39.296 --> 00:02:43.035
The devices are custom
built for this project.

00:02:43.036 --> 00:02:46.460
They have an application
processing unit,

00:02:46.461 --> 00:02:47.860
a microprocessor,

00:02:47.861 --> 00:02:51.490
and a microcontroller in
decreasing order of power.

00:02:51.491 --> 00:02:55.035
There's a one megabyte
on-chip RAM.

00:02:55.036 --> 00:02:57.115
The objective of
this project is to

00:02:57.116 --> 00:02:59.140
classify noise complaints in

00:02:59.141 --> 00:03:00.850
New York City in real-time so

00:03:00.851 --> 00:03:04.190
that real-time actuation
can be done on them.

00:03:04.191 --> 00:03:07.460
Because a city,
an urban canyon is

00:03:07.461 --> 00:03:12.055
highly variable and
dynamic environment

00:03:12.056 --> 00:03:14.460
and the noise conditions,

00:03:14.461 --> 00:03:16.270
the background conditions,
the noise intensity,

00:03:16.271 --> 00:03:17.720
et cetera are all

00:03:17.721 --> 00:03:21.005
extremely variable based on
where you place the devices.

00:03:21.006 --> 00:03:23.960
None of the existing
smaller datasets

00:03:23.961 --> 00:03:27.725
of non-speech sounds were
really enough to train it,

00:03:27.726 --> 00:03:31.620
which is why a cut was

00:03:31.621 --> 00:03:33.100
built from the Google dataset

00:03:33.101 --> 00:03:35.350
to train what we call the Look,

00:03:35.351 --> 00:03:36.910
Listen, and Learn
audio embedding that

00:03:36.911 --> 00:03:38.615
came out from Google last year.

00:03:38.616 --> 00:03:41.960
So here, what we do is we
train an audio sub-network and

00:03:41.961 --> 00:03:44.005
a video sub-network together

00:03:44.006 --> 00:03:45.825
and learn the association
between them,

00:03:45.826 --> 00:03:46.930
are they related or not?

00:03:46.931 --> 00:03:48.410
For example, this is

00:03:48.411 --> 00:03:50.005
an audio clip from a guitar

00:03:50.006 --> 00:03:51.185
and this is a picture
of a guitar,

00:03:51.186 --> 00:03:52.540
are they related or not?

00:03:52.541 --> 00:03:54.280
The advantage is that
there is no need

00:03:54.281 --> 00:03:55.880
for explicit labeling.

00:03:55.881 --> 00:03:57.985
Something that we lack in

00:03:57.986 --> 00:04:01.450
huge quantities of sound
data that we've collected,

00:04:01.451 --> 00:04:03.055
but we're also
implicitly grouping

00:04:03.056 --> 00:04:06.580
similar audios together and

00:04:06.581 --> 00:04:08.430
finally we cut out
this audio sub-network,

00:04:08.431 --> 00:04:10.750
apply it to some smaller
downstream datasets

00:04:10.751 --> 00:04:12.255
such as DCASE or

00:04:12.256 --> 00:04:14.260
the Urban Sound and use

00:04:14.261 --> 00:04:16.765
that as an embedding with
us more MLP classifier.

00:04:16.766 --> 00:04:19.670
Yeah, here the tall poles are
that the embedding alone,

00:04:19.671 --> 00:04:20.965
the audio embedding alone

00:04:20.966 --> 00:04:22.850
is 56 megabytes,
it's pretty huge.

00:04:22.851 --> 00:04:24.265
It can never fit onto

00:04:24.266 --> 00:04:27.700
the 500 kilobyte budget
that we have on

00:04:27.701 --> 00:04:30.250
these devices which
is why we were

00:04:30.251 --> 00:04:31.750
motivated to try
several techniques to

00:04:31.751 --> 00:04:33.575
see how we could shrink them.

00:04:33.576 --> 00:04:35.685
So in pursuing these
two directions,

00:04:35.686 --> 00:04:38.230
we have designed
a few experiments

00:04:38.231 --> 00:04:39.790
in the workshop that we
wanted to explore and

00:04:39.791 --> 00:04:41.950
get results from so that they

00:04:41.951 --> 00:04:43.230
would set the foundation

00:04:43.231 --> 00:04:44.640
for the next steps
in our research.

00:04:44.641 --> 00:04:46.380
So, Pranshu would
next speak about

00:04:46.381 --> 00:04:48.880
the experiments in
the radar domain

00:04:48.881 --> 00:04:50.920
which involves RNNs. Thank you.

00:04:50.921 --> 00:04:52.590
&gt;&gt; Hey everyone. So basically,

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:54.640
the thing was that as
Dhrub pointed out,

00:04:54.641 --> 00:04:57.880
our objective was to try
out the RNN on the human,

00:04:57.881 --> 00:04:59.560
non-human dataset to
see whether we can

00:04:59.561 --> 00:05:01.480
get a competitive accuracy
while getting

00:05:01.481 --> 00:05:03.400
rid off the cost of
feasible competition which

00:05:03.401 --> 00:05:05.660
was present there in
SVM Bonsai technique.

00:05:05.661 --> 00:05:08.420
So the thing is the way that
RNN concept works is that

00:05:08.421 --> 00:05:09.470
let's just say that you have

00:05:09.471 --> 00:05:11.165
a certain batch on
which your training,

00:05:11.166 --> 00:05:13.040
the RNN, it's supposed to

00:05:13.041 --> 00:05:15.195
roll over to a fixed number
of time steps.

00:05:15.196 --> 00:05:17.875
The caveat here is
that all our cuts,

00:05:17.876 --> 00:05:19.020
they obviously are different.

00:05:19.021 --> 00:05:20.750
One is of two seconds,
one is of five seconds,

00:05:20.751 --> 00:05:22.790
one is of 10 seconds,
and so on so forth.

00:05:22.791 --> 00:05:25.220
So, one way to tackle
with this problem

00:05:25.221 --> 00:05:27.979
was that instead of training
directly on the cuts,

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:30.965
you actually artificially
create,a training dataset

00:05:30.966 --> 00:05:32.570
by taking windows out of

00:05:32.571 --> 00:05:34.820
the cut with
an appropriate stride,

00:05:34.821 --> 00:05:37.810
create both a training
and a testing dataset,

00:05:37.811 --> 00:05:39.280
train on the training dataset,

00:05:39.281 --> 00:05:41.735
classify on the testing dataset.

00:05:41.736 --> 00:05:45.280
For getting the accuracy
on a test cut.

00:05:45.281 --> 00:05:47.330
Basically, for all the
windows which had been

00:05:47.331 --> 00:05:49.685
created for this test cut.

00:05:49.686 --> 00:05:52.090
Do something like
majority prediction.

00:05:52.091 --> 00:05:54.530
So, when we tried
out this approach,

00:05:54.531 --> 00:05:57.630
the problem was that for
the test cuts which had

00:05:57.631 --> 00:06:01.240
relatively shorter length of
two seconds or even lower,

00:06:01.241 --> 00:06:03.305
the accuracy was relatively poor.

00:06:03.306 --> 00:06:05.480
So, we are only able to reach

00:06:05.481 --> 00:06:07.150
an accuracy of
roughly round about

00:06:07.151 --> 00:06:12.085
94.8 percent only on the cuts
which were greater than-

00:06:12.086 --> 00:06:14.475
&gt;&gt; Clarify, what you're cutting?

00:06:14.476 --> 00:06:16.590
&gt;&gt; Basically, a cut, a data file.

00:06:16.591 --> 00:06:19.450
&gt;&gt; So, how is this related
to previous two slides,

00:06:19.451 --> 00:06:21.640
which applications
are you looking at?

00:06:21.641 --> 00:06:24.460
Are you looking at
a specific application?

00:06:24.461 --> 00:06:26.220
&gt;&gt; So this is related to
the radar application.

00:06:26.221 --> 00:06:28.570
When an object displaces
in front of the radar,

00:06:28.571 --> 00:06:31.735
we have a displacement detector
that cuts out the section

00:06:31.736 --> 00:06:33.950
of the ambient that

00:06:33.951 --> 00:06:35.945
corresponds to the
displacement of the object.

00:06:35.946 --> 00:06:37.085
We refer to that as a cut.

00:06:37.086 --> 00:06:38.870
Sorry for not clarifying this.

00:06:38.871 --> 00:06:41.335
&gt;&gt; So basically, as I mentioned,

00:06:41.336 --> 00:06:42.680
the problem with
this approach was that

00:06:42.681 --> 00:06:44.565
those tests cuts
for short duration,

00:06:44.566 --> 00:06:47.360
the accuracy on them
was relatively poor.

00:06:47.361 --> 00:06:50.090
Yeah. So basically, we

00:06:50.091 --> 00:06:52.435
tried out a second approach
of dynamic RNN,

00:06:52.436 --> 00:06:54.110
which had the
flexibility that even

00:06:54.111 --> 00:06:55.960
if the cuts were
of varying length,

00:06:55.961 --> 00:06:57.075
in the training dataset,

00:06:57.076 --> 00:06:58.820
it would unroll to the number of

00:06:58.821 --> 00:07:01.905
times of any specific cut.

00:07:01.906 --> 00:07:03.795
Here the first risk was that,

00:07:03.796 --> 00:07:05.950
on the test cut, if you
take the longest length,

00:07:05.951 --> 00:07:10.870
it was exorbitantly
long, of one RNN.

00:07:10.871 --> 00:07:12.095
It's very difficult to train

00:07:12.096 --> 00:07:13.860
as you increase
the number of time steps.

00:07:13.861 --> 00:07:15.325
So what we did was we took

00:07:15.326 --> 00:07:17.745
a sub-cut length from each cut,

00:07:17.746 --> 00:07:20.110
and we had this thing
that in case if

00:07:20.111 --> 00:07:22.650
a cut does not have the length
equal to sub-cut length,

00:07:22.651 --> 00:07:25.250
just simply part it to
become equal to that.

00:07:25.251 --> 00:07:27.335
Now, train directly on
the cuts rather than

00:07:27.336 --> 00:07:29.510
any artificial dataset
and classify

00:07:29.511 --> 00:07:32.245
directly also on
the test cuts themselves.

00:07:32.246 --> 00:07:34.840
This was giving
pretty comparative accuracy

00:07:34.841 --> 00:07:36.430
of 96.5 percent.

00:07:36.431 --> 00:07:39.620
Also, we remove that restriction
that the test cuts,

00:07:39.621 --> 00:07:40.895
they have to be greater than a

00:07:40.896 --> 00:07:42.370
certain length like 2.5 seconds.

00:07:42.371 --> 00:07:45.245
This is for all the
possible test cuts.

00:07:45.246 --> 00:07:49.190
So, I've mentioned some
of the figures here.

00:07:49.191 --> 00:07:50.620
So, that the model size,

00:07:50.621 --> 00:07:52.650
it's reasonably comparative
if you compare,

00:07:52.651 --> 00:07:55.240
especially with the
SVM of 5.7 KB in

00:07:55.241 --> 00:07:57.810
comparison to 30 KB is
larger than the Bonsai,

00:07:57.811 --> 00:07:59.635
but, the thing is that,
we've gotten rid of

00:07:59.636 --> 00:08:02.725
the future that
competition thing.

00:08:02.726 --> 00:08:04.480
So, this is a pretty good result

00:08:04.481 --> 00:08:06.935
because this
illustrates that term,

00:08:06.936 --> 00:08:10.360
without resorting to
more complicated techniques

00:08:10.361 --> 00:08:13.090
like dual RNN and by
avoiding future competition,

00:08:13.091 --> 00:08:14.290
we can get a good accuracy

00:08:14.291 --> 00:08:16.825
with relatively low model size.

00:08:16.826 --> 00:08:20.050
So, these are some of
the advantages as I

00:08:20.051 --> 00:08:21.480
mentioned that only fix sub-cut

00:08:21.481 --> 00:08:23.420
of approximately two
seconds is required.

00:08:23.421 --> 00:08:25.805
This makes way for
faster classification,

00:08:25.806 --> 00:08:30.950
no stacking this thing, to
verify this more efficient.

00:08:30.951 --> 00:08:33.690
So, extending this
sorting further,

00:08:33.691 --> 00:08:35.500
Android data set
which is patently

00:08:35.501 --> 00:08:38.285
a harder task than
the human versus nonhuman.

00:08:38.286 --> 00:08:40.690
We tried out you can see
a number of techniques,

00:08:40.691 --> 00:08:42.970
like you can say SVM, Bonsai,

00:08:42.971 --> 00:08:44.755
DeepBonsai and this thing,

00:08:44.756 --> 00:08:46.775
and irrespective of
the technique used,

00:08:46.776 --> 00:08:48.160
it's giving an accuracy

00:08:48.161 --> 00:08:49.925
of you can say,
roughly 80 percent.

00:08:49.926 --> 00:08:52.780
So, the one good thing which
will illustrate [inaudible]

00:08:52.781 --> 00:08:54.595
is roughly you can
say competitive

00:08:54.596 --> 00:08:56.685
as compared to other techniques
on the android data set.

00:08:56.686 --> 00:08:59.510
But the larger implication was
that there's a shortage of

00:08:59.511 --> 00:09:02.430
data in this particular task
and we need more data.

00:09:02.431 --> 00:09:05.355
The possessor of that data
collection is continuing on,

00:09:05.356 --> 00:09:08.785
and I am hopeful that
in the next 1-2 months,

00:09:08.786 --> 00:09:10.350
we get an access and make

00:09:10.351 --> 00:09:14.060
better inroads into so exercise.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:20.075
&gt;&gt; We have a C-sharp
implementation of the RNN,

00:09:20.076 --> 00:09:21.570
which is what our motor runs on.

00:09:21.571 --> 00:09:23.940
So, it uses a paired
down version of

00:09:23.941 --> 00:09:26.470
Microsoft.net micro framework
that we call the e-mode,

00:09:26.471 --> 00:09:29.095
but the application programmer
writes code in C-sharp.

00:09:29.096 --> 00:09:30.590
We could not demonstrate
for you today,

00:09:30.591 --> 00:09:31.760
and we apologize because we do

00:09:31.761 --> 00:09:33.305
not have the hardware
board with us,

00:09:33.306 --> 00:09:35.230
it did not reach us in time.

00:09:35.231 --> 00:09:39.320
But it is developed
fully unit tested,

00:09:39.321 --> 00:09:40.820
and we can start doing

00:09:40.821 --> 00:09:43.395
field tests as soon as we
get back from this workshop.

00:09:43.396 --> 00:09:45.680
&gt;&gt; Shriram, this is
the paper we've submitted

00:09:45.681 --> 00:09:48.395
to NIPS this time,

00:09:48.396 --> 00:09:51.430
and they don't a focus

00:09:51.431 --> 00:09:53.520
on the classifiers so
there's no proton in Bonsai,

00:09:53.521 --> 00:09:55.470
but there's a completely
new architecture for doing

00:09:55.471 --> 00:09:58.560
feature computation
and time series.

00:09:58.561 --> 00:10:00.750
&gt;&gt; Sorry, how much
time do we have?

00:10:00.751 --> 00:10:01.470
&gt;&gt; Thirty more minutes.

00:10:01.471 --> 00:10:03.630
&gt;&gt; Okay. Yeah, I'll
be slightly fast.

00:10:03.631 --> 00:10:05.280
So, now we go to

00:10:05.281 --> 00:10:09.990
the sonic project of

00:10:09.991 --> 00:10:12.115
tackling noise complaints
in New York City.

00:10:12.116 --> 00:10:14.220
So, to remind you
of the motivation,

00:10:14.221 --> 00:10:18.585
we have to fit the system at
a relatively big classifier,

00:10:18.586 --> 00:10:21.930
the L3 embedding onto
a 500 kilobyte budget.

00:10:21.931 --> 00:10:25.165
So, we took two directions
to explore this question.

00:10:25.166 --> 00:10:27.215
One was, can RNNs

00:10:27.216 --> 00:10:29.260
work out-of-the-box to
tackle the problem?

00:10:29.261 --> 00:10:31.095
Do we even need an embedding for

00:10:31.096 --> 00:10:33.895
a non speech acoustic events?

00:10:33.896 --> 00:10:36.730
So, we tried extensively with

00:10:36.731 --> 00:10:40.035
single-layered or
stacked GRUs and LSTMs,

00:10:40.036 --> 00:10:43.305
and some variants
such as FastGRNN,

00:10:43.306 --> 00:10:46.940
and we used the
UrbanSound 8K data set,

00:10:46.941 --> 00:10:50.070
which had around 200,000
training samples.

00:10:50.071 --> 00:10:53.630
These are some of the
hyper-parameters we played with,

00:10:53.631 --> 00:10:55.315
and some of the model
variance we played with.

00:10:55.316 --> 00:10:57.650
So, in the first
phase, we compute

00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:02.690
a Mel-spectrogram
of 199 timesteps

00:11:02.691 --> 00:11:04.060
across 256 filter bands.

00:11:04.061 --> 00:11:05.710
This is used as input to the RNN.

00:11:05.711 --> 00:11:08.360
We optionally have
a second layer of RNN,

00:11:08.361 --> 00:11:11.025
if we looked into stacking,

00:11:11.026 --> 00:11:13.525
and finally, we have
10 output classes.

00:11:13.526 --> 00:11:16.020
So, we tried various
hidden dimensions

00:11:16.021 --> 00:11:17.745
dropout rates, learning
rates et cetera,

00:11:17.746 --> 00:11:20.305
and a few variants
of RNNs such as,

00:11:20.306 --> 00:11:23.790
low-rank FastGRNNs
and stacked RNNs.

00:11:23.791 --> 00:11:28.040
So, we observe that
the main issue with

00:11:28.041 --> 00:11:30.350
RNNs in tackling
non-physical events is

00:11:30.351 --> 00:11:32.870
that they suffer from
severe over fitting,

00:11:32.871 --> 00:11:35.590
and no matter what we
did we couldn't bridge

00:11:35.591 --> 00:11:39.890
the train test gap
closer than 30 percent.

00:11:39.891 --> 00:11:41.640
For example, this is some of

00:11:41.641 --> 00:11:43.905
the best results
we got using RNNs,

00:11:43.906 --> 00:11:46.845
92 percent accuracy in 60 epochs,

00:11:46.846 --> 00:11:48.490
and by this time,

00:11:48.491 --> 00:11:49.570
the training saturates, so we

00:11:49.571 --> 00:11:50.800
didn't really continue further.

00:11:50.801 --> 00:11:53.660
But the validation accuracy
is like 63 percent,

00:11:53.661 --> 00:11:56.435
even though the model says
that's sufficiently small,

00:11:56.436 --> 00:11:58.060
if it would have worked,

00:11:58.061 --> 00:12:00.700
it would have been great
for us, but it didn't.

00:12:00.701 --> 00:12:02.635
So, we track played around with

00:12:02.636 --> 00:12:04.655
various input representations
such as using

00:12:04.656 --> 00:12:07.110
regular spectrograms instead
of mel-spectrograms,

00:12:07.111 --> 00:12:09.775
that did even worse.

00:12:09.776 --> 00:12:12.410
We found that stacking
doesn't help and FastGRNN

00:12:12.411 --> 00:12:13.580
only does marginally better

00:12:13.581 --> 00:12:16.040
because it has fewer parameters.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:19.230
So, we have pretty much concluded

00:12:19.231 --> 00:12:22.800
that RNNs alone are not
competitive with CNNs,

00:12:22.801 --> 00:12:25.380
which is why in
our present exploration,

00:12:25.381 --> 00:12:26.965
while we're trying to train

00:12:26.966 --> 00:12:29.645
an equivalent student model
from the L3 embedding,

00:12:29.646 --> 00:12:31.700
we did not exactly use

00:12:31.701 --> 00:12:34.475
vanilla RNN as
our candidate student model,

00:12:34.476 --> 00:12:36.890
rather we used a combination

00:12:36.891 --> 00:12:39.155
of convolution and recurrence.

00:12:39.156 --> 00:12:43.460
So, for knowledge
distillation purposes,

00:12:43.461 --> 00:12:46.580
we used a variant of the student
model with convolutions

00:12:46.581 --> 00:12:49.805
and another variant with
RNNs as candidate models,

00:12:49.806 --> 00:12:52.110
and we trained on
the same UrbanSound 8k.

00:12:52.111 --> 00:12:53.980
This is not ideal,

00:12:53.981 --> 00:12:55.230
and I'll tell you
why in a second,

00:12:55.231 --> 00:12:58.015
but given the time
left in the workshop,

00:12:58.016 --> 00:13:01.150
we could only train
on the UrbanSound 8k.

00:13:01.151 --> 00:13:04.030
So, this is an example
of this urban model.

00:13:04.031 --> 00:13:07.225
We are now learning
from the raw sample.

00:13:07.226 --> 00:13:11.575
So, if we're sampling at
48 kilohertz, this is the input.

00:13:11.576 --> 00:13:13.470
We are passing it through a layer

00:13:13.471 --> 00:13:16.385
of convolution filters, 64,

00:13:16.386 --> 00:13:18.310
and then in the CNN variant,

00:13:18.311 --> 00:13:19.560
we are passing it
through a bunch of

00:13:19.561 --> 00:13:22.305
two de-convolution filters
that vary from two to four.

00:13:22.306 --> 00:13:23.960
In the RNN case, we're passing it

00:13:23.961 --> 00:13:26.070
through two stack layers,

00:13:26.071 --> 00:13:29.320
and again, we're mapping them
to the 10 output classes.

00:13:29.321 --> 00:13:33.400
While the CNN does not give
us great accuracy, in fact,

00:13:33.401 --> 00:13:35.890
it is comparable with
the accuracy that we

00:13:35.891 --> 00:13:39.630
get with the vanilla
RNN approach,

00:13:39.631 --> 00:13:41.065
and it also has a fatter model,

00:13:41.066 --> 00:13:42.635
which all three of them do.

00:13:42.636 --> 00:13:47.860
We are starting to see some
good results with simple GRU,

00:13:47.861 --> 00:13:50.270
the GRU variant of simple net.

00:13:50.271 --> 00:13:52.480
It is 65 percent accuracy,

00:13:52.481 --> 00:13:54.080
and it is staying
pretty constant at

00:13:54.081 --> 00:13:56.240
62 percent to 65 percent,

00:13:56.241 --> 00:13:58.990
at least in the box that
we have trained so far.

00:13:58.991 --> 00:14:04.510
Again, we're competing with
the target of 77 percent,

00:14:04.511 --> 00:14:06.630
there is still some gap
to be breached here.

00:14:06.631 --> 00:14:08.650
However, we have
trained it on one of

00:14:08.651 --> 00:14:10.080
the downstream data sets due to

00:14:10.081 --> 00:14:11.925
lack of time this was not ideal,

00:14:11.926 --> 00:14:13.655
because the L3 embedding is

00:14:13.656 --> 00:14:15.490
really trained from
the big Google data set.

00:14:15.491 --> 00:14:17.650
So, one of the first task
that we will do when

00:14:17.651 --> 00:14:20.650
we return from the workshop is to

00:14:20.651 --> 00:14:22.730
train these existing
student models with

00:14:22.731 --> 00:14:24.200
the original Google data that we

00:14:24.201 --> 00:14:25.885
were used to train
the L3 embedding,

00:14:25.886 --> 00:14:28.610
and we think that we should
be able to get a lot more

00:14:28.611 --> 00:14:32.350
out of it at a much smaller size.

00:14:32.351 --> 00:14:34.570
The second deduction that

00:14:34.571 --> 00:14:35.860
we want to explore
as the next step

00:14:35.861 --> 00:14:37.890
is developing
new student architectures

00:14:37.891 --> 00:14:40.600
for non speech acoustics sensing.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:44.000
One of the ideas that we
have been discussing is,

00:14:44.001 --> 00:14:46.650
there are some existing
compression techniques

00:14:46.651 --> 00:14:48.440
that reduced model size.

00:14:48.441 --> 00:14:50.920
For example, through removing
redundancies in the model.

00:14:50.921 --> 00:14:53.170
One of them is called deepIOT,

00:14:53.171 --> 00:14:55.580
which these two are good
enough compression.

00:14:55.581 --> 00:14:59.370
However, it comes with
the cost of accuracy drop,

00:14:59.371 --> 00:15:02.310
and we can use that as
a student to buy back

00:15:02.311 --> 00:15:03.800
some of their accuracy
drop hopefully

00:15:03.801 --> 00:15:05.490
from the original teacher model,

00:15:05.491 --> 00:15:08.790
and then once the student
has been fixed,

00:15:08.791 --> 00:15:10.350
we can continue further with

00:15:10.351 --> 00:15:11.500
modal compression techniques such

00:15:11.501 --> 00:15:13.525
as convolution kernel separation,

00:15:13.526 --> 00:15:15.830
deep compression
pipeline, et cetera.

00:15:15.831 --> 00:15:17.935
In conclusion, we
would like to thank

00:15:17.936 --> 00:15:19.310
Microsoft very much for

00:15:19.311 --> 00:15:20.610
this opportunity that
they have given us.

00:15:20.611 --> 00:15:23.725
It was great to work and
interact with all of you.

00:15:23.726 --> 00:15:26.147
Special thanks to
Harsha, Prateek, Aditya,

00:15:26.148 --> 00:15:29.860
Professor Monic and Don,

00:15:29.861 --> 00:15:31.150
and with that, I thank you all

00:15:31.151 --> 00:15:33.320
and open floor to questions.

